# -*- encoding: utf-8 -*-

"""Typos captured after the rules were written, and kept away from them.

`cases.py` is the corpus the rules were tuned against: a regression list, and
a fair one, but most of its tool messages share a first line with some rule's
own fixture, and three of its expectations were rewritten to what the engine
already said. A number measured on it says the rules do what they were written
to do. This file is the other number.

Every case was captured on 2026-10-13 by running the failing command in a
container -- the source of each is the last field -- and its expectation was
written down before The Bleep was run on it. The rules this file found wrong
were fixed in the commits that followed, so the number moved because of it;
from here on, the discipline is what keeps it honest:

- an expectation is never changed to agree with the engine. A wrong
  expectation is fixed by hand, in review, and says why;
- an expectation may be a tuple when more than one answer is right -- chiefly
  the same correction with the user's own flags kept (`mvn -o -q compile` for
  `mvn -o -q compil`);
- no test under `tests/rules` reads this file. A rule fixed because of a case
  here gets its own fixture there, captured afresh.

Which captures are here is mechanical: every word the expected answer adds to
the command is in what the program printed, on the fixed `PATH` in
`executables.txt`, or a shell word (`&&`, `sudo`, `cd`, `mkdir -p`). A typo
whose answer is a branch name, a file in the directory or an option only
`--help` lists is left out, because offline -- `bench/hit_rate.py` answers
these with no tool asked and in an empty directory -- it could only measure
that nothing was asked. Those are what the compatibility matrix and typing at
real shells are for.

"""

